WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.979
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.979 --> 00:00:10.549
Style transfer is an amazing area in machine
learning and AI research, where we take two

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:11.549
images.

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:17.040
Image number one is an input photograph, and
image number two, is the desired style.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.490
And the output of this process is the content
of image number one with the style of image

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:23.490
number two.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.680
This first paper opened up an incredible new
area of research.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.110
As a result, a ton of different variants have
emerged in the last two years.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:37.399
Feedforward style transfer for close to real-time
results, temporally coherent style transfer

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:40.039
for videos, and much, much more.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:44.969
And this one not only outperforms previously
existing techniques, but also broadens the

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:48.390
horizon of possible style transfer applications.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.690
And obviously, a human would be best at doing
this because a human has an understanding

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.399
of the objects seen in these images.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:01.429
And now, hold on to your papers, because the
main objective of this is method to create

00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:04.549
semantically meaningful results for style
transfer.

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:10.500
It is meant to do well with input image pairs
that may look completely different visually,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.920
but have some semantic components that are
similar.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.700
For instance, a photograph of a human face
and a drawing of a virtual character is excellent

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.020
example of that.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:26.649
In this case, this learning algorithm recognizes
that they both have noses and uses this valuable

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:29.430
information in the style transfer process.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.950
As a result, it has three super cool applications.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:39.380
First, the regular photo to style transfer
that we all know and love.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:51.270
Second, it is also capable of swapping the
style of two input images.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:58.659
Third, and hold on to your papers because
this is going to be even more insane.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:03.579
Style or sketch to photo.

00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:10.179
And we have a plus one here as well, so, fourth,
it also supports color transfer between photographs,

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:13.340
which will allow creating amazing time-lapse
videos.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.740
I always try to lure you Fellow Scholars into
looking at these papers, so make sure to have

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.370
a look at the paper for some more results
on this.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.060
And you can see here that this method was
compared to several other techniques, for

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.440
instance, you can see the cycle consistency
paper and PatchMatch.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.730
And this is one of those moments when I get
super happy, because more than a 170 episodes

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.489
into the series, we can not only appreciate
the quality of these these new results, but

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:44.360
we also had previous episodes about both of
these algorithms.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.480
As always, the links are available in the
video description, make sure to have a look,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.250
it's going to be a lot of fun.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.909
The source code of this project is also available.

00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:57.400
We also have a ton of episodes on computer
graphics in the series, make sure to have

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.700
a look at those as well.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:04.519
Every now and then I get e-mails from viewers
who say that they came for the AI videos,

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:10.500
and just in case, watched a recent episode
on computer graphics and were completely hooked.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

